# Every text file is stored AND checked out with LF, on every OS.
#
# Why: the test-suite reads tracked files byte-for-byte (.env.example,
# phpunit.xml, workflows, Blade views, ...) and asserts on them with
# regular expressions. With core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout turns
# `KEY=` into `KEY=\r`, and `/^KEY=.+/m` then matches the `\r`, so a test
# that is green on Linux CI fails on the Windows working tree. Pinning the
# working-tree EOL here makes that class of failure impossible instead of
# fixing it one file at a time. Git still detects binaries (png, ttf, zip,
# the Rust binary, ...) itself and never converts them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch scripts are the one exception: cmd.exe needs CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
